\f1\fs22 The Johnny was buzzing, his hands clammy shaking from a cocktail of endorphins, adrenaline and grand ambition . His team had made a discovery of proportions unimagined. If he milked circumstance and misrepresented himself, his name would last forever. It had been an arduous task for his team, but they had completed the analysis of his last, impromptu and near disastrous experiment. The results were simply staggering. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Taking credit for this will be my greatest achievement.\'94 He accidentally said out loud, instantly regretting his enthusiasm as his eyes darted about to confirm no-one had heard. The appointment with Sarah was only a few minutes away and he readied himself for the accolades that he would, no doubt, be buried in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Johnny immediately relented. \'93My experiment with centrifugal force upon the discs has yielded some interesting results. The spinning of both discs created a gravity well and our simulations confirm it would have grown, at an exponential rate, becoming a small black hole. The earth was at risk of being consumed before I managed to stop it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93This child stopped it, not you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That is not the way I remember it.\'94 His could not process facts that challenged his self importance. \'93I draw your attention to the holo-screen you will notice images taken from the inside of the bunker. Burnt into the walls and ceiling are familiar patterns. Or are they? Looking closely you may notice that there is one extra line.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I would expect the pattern to be the same. Aren\'92t they supposed to be a representation of the gravity waves at the time of the big bang? Why would they change?\'94 Sarah was confused, but intrigued.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I have not worked that one out yet, but this is something that will interest you. You will note that the patterns appear to be a many short fuzzy, oscillating lines randomly placed, but all falling within a generally circular pattern. I have had my techs working night and day and I think they are visible representations of digital signals, coded messages, not random oscillations. And there are hundreds of them!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That is impossible.\'94 Sarah stared off into space. \'93Messages from the time of the big bang?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Before the big bang.\'94 The Johnny then took a deep breath. \'93Messages from God.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Again impossible.\'94 Sarah reiterated. \'93And you cannot jump to that conclusion, there must be many equally valid explanations.\'94 Sarah was dumbfounded, but could not readily think of another explanation. 
\f2\i God cannot be the answer. It simply cannot be so, Sarah kept to herself.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley needed to bring him down a notch. \'93It seems a bit pointless if we don\'92t know what the messages say, if they are indeed messages.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We could be looking at signals from before the beginning of time.\'94 The Johnny let weight of the announcement settle before continuing, \'93This could be the most important break-through in history. Or histories.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, if it is what you claim, I will ensure your team gets the credit.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s my team! My discovery. My destiny!!\'94 Johnny\'92s imagined Nobel Prize had been snatched away, just when it could be seen so clearly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Dr Logan has the innoculant, but slow down a bit. How long until you can confirm what they are? \'94 Hayley needed to take control. \'93I don\'92t want you doing any more tests until you have a reconstructed message or until I am satisfied they are not messages at all. If they are, they might give us some clues as to how to deal with these wretched discs.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I have my subordinates working on that problem.\'94 Johnny countered \'93I am free to do what I want. My team, my\'85.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No! Not your team, not your discovery, not your destiny. The department owns you and everything you do. I am the Director of this idiotic venture and you will do my bidding. If you do not pull yourself into line, I will terminate your contract and it will be agonising. Is that the what you want?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, no it is not.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I understand.\'94 Johnny was close to tears. 10 minutes prior he was clutching a Nobel Prize.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Dr Logan will stay until my return.\'94 Sarah announced. \'93Kathy? Take custody of the discs and make sure they reconstruct the messages before planning anymore experiments.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>As much as he loved proximity to Sarah, Cracker had not joined her on the second trip to the lab, but arranged to meet her later that day in Jamison Hardy\'92s Operations Centre. He was still hopeful of accessing the riches Jamison had squirreled away those many years ago, but his knowledge of ancient computer code was minimal. Fucking up the entire system would be the inevitable result of his limited skill set clashing with the old-tech. Sarah was the only one who could help.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He had managed to find an alternate entrance to Jamison Hardy\'92s opulent apartment, avoiding the necessity and hassle of stealing past the heavy security of the exclusive Gravity Well Restaurant. Through an ordeal of trial and error, mostly error, over several weeks of late night jaunts, he blundered across the tycoon\'92s private elevator and now enjoyed easy access from street level. He had all but moved into the apartment, but found himself lonely among the finery. He had never felt lonely in the Pale, there was alway someone screaming or someone stabbing someone for screaming. For that reason he had always coveted solitude, but right now, solitude sucked dick. He wanted to show off his 7-star accommodations, he wanted to be one of those painful new-rich, unable to control ostentatious displays of wealth. 
\f2\i One day soon. 
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Although he was keen to remove every credit from the vast pile Jamison\'92s computers had collected, he had taken on board Sarah\'92s stark warning that messing nsequences to the world economy. She had been with the program may have dire colargely absent from Cracker\'92s world in the weeks leading up to the birth of Richard, but even with a fleeting glimpse of the data-base, she was certain the program controlled a significant chunk of the world debt. It was impossible to predict how, exactly, economies would falter and Sarah was reluctant to tinker and provoke a cascade of losses. In that eventuality the wealthy would, no doubt, throw a tantrum costing thousands, perhaps millions of lives. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He entered the control room but, forbidden to touch anything, was immediately bored. He wandered aimlessly, opening and shutting doors of empty cabinets, pulling out drawers and rifling through the few items left behind. After only a few minutes he had pretty much exhausted all possible sticky-beaking opportunities and had one last door to open. As the final door creaked open, he entered a large, walk in cupboard containing every cleaning product any self respecting commercial cleaner would ever need. There was even an arcane mop and bucket, the utility of which was not lost on Cracker, long desperate to clear the multi-layered dust accumulated by time. He and the mop made their way around the floor with speed and expertise, replacing blackened water and chemical at regular intervals. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He adeptly danced with the mop, gliding across the floor backwards swiping figure 8\'92s, each stroke slicing through the filth and unveiling hardwood of glowing Burgundy. He cleared wide swathes up and down the control room, glancing through the transparent doorways at every pass and noted \'93Gravity Well\'94 had just started luncheon service. Lunch was elegantly chaotic, with unsmiling patrons waiting impatiently, neuroses on standby, for something worthy of complaint. Any misstep would see personality disorders flare up and contrive to destroy the dim-witted wait-staff. For some reason the inferior did not seem to comprehend or acknowledge their lowliness and needed regular reminders. 
\f2\i Keep the common in their place and keep the company in the black.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Curious to take a closer look at the freak show unfolding before him, Cracker approached the second of the portals. Through it he could see that several waiters were on the receiving end of loud and intense abuse from various patrons. One waitress, it seemed, had been unable to predict what wine the patron wanted and was suffering from her lack of clairvoyance. These employees were no level 1 scum, either. To occupy the position of waiter in \'91Gravity Well\'92, one had to be at least level 3 and often as high as level 4. Even so, the executive classes looked upon them with an intensity of hate only wealthy hearts could generate. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Perhaps it was his loneliness or his natural dislike of these prancing ponies but Cracker\'92s impulse control abandoned him. Without any particular plan, without even thinking, he found himself walking through the shimmering doorway and into the finest dining room on the planet. Three or four fast paced steps had him standing before a flabby level 6 executive.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What the hell are you?\'94 The bloater bellowed. \'93Where did you come from?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Cracker of the Hardy clan.\'94 Cracker punched him in the face and watched as the bubble of lard flopped about on the floor, desperately trying to right himself. Security sprinted towards him, but Cracker was already headed for his private exit and the door was open and closed before anyone could intervene. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Several perplexed guards and the matre\'91d (?) bashed at the wall with their fists, but were met with solid stone. One security guard produced his P.P.P and let off a few pulses into the wall, but with no effect. The stone did not even chip, while on the other side, Cracker gave them a one finger salute, followed by a traditional and heart-felt brown-eye.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What are you up to, Great Teacher?\'94 said a voice from behind. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Teaching them a lesson.\'94 Cracker turned to see the Blessed Mother with Richard wedged to her hip and a small team of Elvis guards following behind. \'93Good, you found the entrance. It wasn\'92t too hard to find I hope. No Blessed Aunt Kathy?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, she is trying to stop Johnny destroying the world. What is that all about?\'94 She asked, looking through the doorway and into Gravity Well. \'93Are they shooting at us?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah, I guess they are.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What did you do?\'94 Asked Sarah, in the manner a dried up Headmistress might question a child, just prior to the beating.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93See fat-arse in the corner? The one spitting up teeth?\'94 Cracker asked, not requiring an answer. \'93He was screaming at the poor waitress. It was terrible, so I popped him one.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That large gentleman I recognise as CFO of Primary Trade Dominion Gas and Oil.\'94 She said before finding herself distracted by the fate of the waitress. \'93Nice work. It looks like she\'92s been fired. Security is marching her to the door.\'94 Sarah pointed out. \'93You should act more prudently. Uninformed action, can have unexpected consequences.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Level 5\'92s and 6\'92s don\'92t have to control themselves. Why should I be expected to behave any better?\'94 He said as he watched the waitress disappear. \'93Okay, yeah, I have buyer\'92s remorse. I will make it up to her, I promise.\'94 He was yet to discover that rich men need not keep their promises. Making them was gift enough.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That is why I expect better of you.\'94 She replied. \'93You actually care, don\'92t you?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I guess that\'92s why I belted him.\'94 Cracker said, \'93Well your visit is timely, Blessed Mother, I am expecting the High Elvis and his entourage. They wish to pay homage to Richard.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, he contacted me a little while ago.\'94 Sarah replied. \'93He is coming with his entire cabinet.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I knew I was having a couple of guests, but I didn\'92t know I was throwing a party.\'94 
\f2\i Things are starting to look up.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, what have you prepared for refreshments?\'94 She looked about and shrugged her shoulders. \'93Nothing, I see.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I\'92ll just grab some fruit, although I won\'92t be having any today.\'94 Cracker loved the fresh fruit, so much so he had made the unfortunate discovery that an exclusive diet of fruit turned his arse into a fire hose. \'93Yeah, I might stick with red wine and cheese, mostly cheese.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis and his cabinet arrived and were ushered into the library wing and seated at a wooden table of ornately carved walnut. Sarah, Richard and her Elvi followed closely behind.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Books! Books made of paper!\'94 The Blessed Mother was shocked. \'93These books must be priceless. Look at them! Beautifully bound and gleaming. This is my idea of heaven.\'94 Drawn to the shelves of neatly stacked hard backs of every kind, she opened books at random and nose-dived into the pages to deeply draw in the scent of printers ink and bleached paper. \'93They\'92ve never been opened! What a tragedy. No one have ever read them?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Take what you like.\'94 Cracker kindly offered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You don\'92t actually own any of this, not yet anyway.\'94 Sarah warned. \'93At the moment you are merely the most successful squatter in history.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay, I will keep them myself.\'94 He did not know what he would use them for. Reading them certainly wasn\'92t high on the agenda. 
\f2\i Door stop, perhaps? Fat Frisbee?
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Blessed Mother we have much to discuss, but first may I hold the great one?\'94 The High Elvis asked, hoping not to be denied.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Of Course,\'94 Sarah handed over Richard, who kicked his legs about in delight.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It is indeed a blessing to hold the One-after-the-One. All Hail our Saviour!\'94 The High Elvis held the child aloft and the Cabinet bowed in silent adoration. \'93All hail The Dawkins.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Praise be unto him\'94 The Elvis Cabinet sang out with pure joy. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What do you wish to discuss H.E.?\'94 Sarah decided saying High Elvis all of the time was far too formal, especially for the Blessed Mother. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The celebration of the birth of our Saviour. The Elvi nation is still largely ignorant of his existence and this cannot continue. Only a lucky few know of the blessed event and even though we have managed to keep it from common knowledge, it cannot stand. I would argue that it shouldn\'92t. We must celebrate this wonderful news and it must be done very soon. The Elvi are not fools and must realise that the birth is long overdue.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I want the Elvi to have their celebration, too, but I do not want to place Richard in peril. The P.T.D. know of him, I mean there are several million \'91The one-after-the-one is coming\'92 posters glued to every building in the Pale. Elvis concerts have been replete with songs and monologues about \'91The Dawkins\'92 for many months and we know the P.T.D. are plotting to kill off the Elvi nation. How are you going to protect him, once his identity is known?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The first thing is to ensure his identity is not discovered, not until he is much older, but we must continue to spread his legend. He cannot be allowed to fade from memory. You and Richard will have, at your disposal, all the resources of the Elvi Nation. We do not have much capital to spare, much has been diverted to Graceland Security, but we have the man-power to guarantee your security indefinitely. Your current arrangements are temporary and we need a long term plan, preferably involving a clandestine safe-house.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I already have that covered.\'94 Sarah said, thinking of her new Directorship at the Lab. \'93Yes, I have a bomb-proof bunker to hide in and the living quarters will be completed in a matter of weeks. We just need to survive until then. My concern is the P.T.D. identifying Richard as the One-after-the-One.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You could have called him something other than Richard-Dawkins.\'94 The High Elvis noted with a touch of sarcasm. \'93I will make arrangements. We will leak some disinformation. A few red herrings here and there should thin out their resources.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I understand the Elvi deserve their prize. They have treated me with such kindness and respect and I will be forever grateful.\'94 Sarah said. \'93I can\'92t stop you having a celebration, but I do not think Richard should be present. The CEO knows of Richard\'92s existence, but not his identity and has asked our friend Hayley, Holy Aunt Hayley, to hunt down the Saviour.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93She won\'92t compromise his safety, will she?\'94 H.E. was horrified at the prospect. 
\f2\i Surely she will not turn on us so easily?
\f1\i0  \'93She is venerated in our community and the entire Elvis continuum would be deeply wounded if she placed Richard in peril.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If she misses out on the Commissioner\'92s job, there is no telling how she will react.\'94 Sarah said with a note of dismay. \'93She is very much a career woman.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable> * * * * * * *\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We have a very strong field for this job, Superintendent.\'94 The CEO announced. \'93We even have a Level 6 contender. The upper executive classes will need a very good reason to overlook him.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Raise my taxes and garnish my super! Why would a level 6 stoop so low?\'94 Hayley thought it absurd that she coveted this job more than anything, but for a \'93sixer\'94 it was a retrograde step. \'93He or she must have their reasons, but I have never known an altruistic upper executive.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Whatever the motivation, the application has been transmitted and is being processed like all of the others.\'94 The CEO enjoyed making his junior executive squirm. \'93You can make your bid for the job by identifying this so-called Saviour of the lower classes\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The One-after-the-One?\'94 Hayley snapped back. \'93That should not be too hard, but why don\'92t you put your Level 6 applicant onto it? If he\'92s such a genius it won\'92t take long.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A challenge? Perhaps you do not think you can do it yourself?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do not try such pitiful mind games, you insult me and demean yourself.\'94 She wasn\'92t going to suck his dick to get the job, that was for certain.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ha! Okay, perhaps we might have ourselves a contest!\'94 The CEO was intrigued. 
\f2\i What mischief will unfold if I agree? 
\f1\i0 \'93How about a direct competition? Whomsoever finds the One-after-the-One shall be the Commissioner.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s not what I had in mind, sir.\'94 Hayley managed to sputter. 
\f2\i What just happened? Shit!. 
\f1\i0 \'93And when I find him? What shall we do with him?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He or she will be killed, of course. This impostor is a symbol of hope. HOPE!!\'94 The CEO raised the volume. \'93Hope is something no-one, no low, is permitted to hold or even to crave. They must learn that hope melts away as soon as their grimy hands grasp it. \'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How do you propose to arrange this little grudge match?\'94 Hayley asked gingerly, hoping the CEO saw the absurdity of the suggestion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well ,I will swear him in as a Chief Superintendent and give him access to the same resources you have. After that it\'92s up to you two. In fact, why don\'92t you two meet and work it out? The CEO smiled and waved his hand over the console on his desk and the holographic head of his personal assistant was immediately floating in front of him. \'93Darlene. Could you please contact our level 6 applicant? And get him into my office?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, sir.\'94 She replied. \'93It should not be more that 5 minutes. He has just finished the medical.\'94 The floating head popped out of existence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You can wait a couple of minutes, can\'92t you Hayley?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Why not?\'94 She said. \'93I am interested to find out why a person of his stature would want to be in the public service.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I think he will peak more than just your interest.\'94 The CEO said. 
\f2\i This ought to be fucking interesting. 
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>After only a few moments of excruciating silence, the hologram of Darlene again made an appearance, \'93Sir, he\'92s here.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, let him in\'94 The said the CEO as the double doors of leading to his outer office slowly opened. \'93Detective Chief Superintendent Hayley Lanning, I think you know Haloren Smythe, Director, L6.\'94 The recently released accused mass murderer entered the office.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93He is the applicant? A serial killer?\'94 Hayley groaned loudly. \'93This is your front-runner? A murderer!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now, now. The prosecution service had to drop the case, it seems someone leaked the arrest video to the media. Did you ever find out who leaked it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, we did not.\'94 Hayley said, keeping a lid on her desire to scream. \'93That should not effect the court case, there is more than enough evidence. We have a recorded confession.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Not good enough!\'94 The CEO snapped. \'93You thought you could sink a level six with a lousy confession? Not nearly good enough.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I told you what would happen to me if you brought me in.\'94 Haloren said. \'93You should have killed me when you had the chance.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My integrity is worth more that some petty act of vengeance.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That is you tragic flaw, Chief Superintendent.\'94 The CEO chimed in. \'93You are inflexible and the upper executive class need a Commissioner who is entirely more pliable.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You want a crook.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Now you are getting it.\'94 The CEO grinned as he turned to Smythe. \'93Haloren, the Chief Superintendent here has proposed something marvelous. A contest!\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>* * **************************************************************\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Haloren Smythe?\'94 Sarah asked incredulously. \'93You are in a contest with a serial killer to find and execute my son?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93A serial killer with the resources and investigative capability of the entire P.T.D. Police department.\'94 Halyley quipped. \'93Trust me, he will find Richard.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How is it that you went to that meeting as the Commissioner-in-waiting and end up drawing straws to kill Richard?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It was certainly an unexpected turn of events. I challenged his ability to conduct a criminal investigation and the CEO just ran with it.\'94 Hayley knew she had planted the idea in he head but decided, quite correctly, Sarah would not be impressed. \'93The next thing I knew I was in a contest. I could not pull out, I would have lost the Commissioner\'92s job on the spot.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I would hate the welfare of my son to get in the way on your career.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93If I had withdrawn from the contest, do you think they would have stopped looking for Richard?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No, of course not.\'94 Sarah conceded. \'93The danger to Richard seemed distant and intangible until you put a name to it. Haloren Smythe. No matter what, we protect Richard at all costs. Agreed?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Do you really need to ask?\'94 Hayley was a little hurt. \'93I will protect Richard with my last breath. I have seen what he can do. He is special, very special indeed.\'94\
